{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792272484,"nonce":"xVzaTIUXB7+NjT83xXoDkDyFVC0=","title":"`C.field++` on a static of a class declared later in the file is lowered as Dynamic arithmetic and corrupts the value","message":"Repro (rayzor run --release, Cranelift and LLVM):\n\n    class Other {\n        public static function post() { V2.a++; }\n        public static function pre() { ++V2.b; }\n        public static function plus() { V2.c += 1; }\n    }\n    class V2 {\n        public static var a = 0; public static var b = 0; public static var c = 0;\n        static function main() {\n            for (i in 0...3) { Other.post(); Other.pre(); Other.plus(); }\n            trace(a + \" \" + b + \" \" + c);   // 1161827216 1161827184 3, expected 3 3 3\n        }\n    }\n\nThe MIR of `Other.post` loads the i32 global, casts it to `*u8`, boxes the 1, and calls the runtime's Dynamic add. So the operand of `++` is typed Dynamic: `V2` comes after `Other` in the file and is not resolved yet. `+= 1` on the same field is typed Int and works. Reads and plain writes of `V2.a` from `Other` work too.\n\nFound while writing haxe-conformance/regressions/TreeFusion.hx, which uses `+= 1` to avoid it.","files":null}]}